The `escapeJavaStyleString` method in the `StringEscapeUtils` class is responsible for performing the actual escaping of characters in a Java string. Below is a snippet of the `escapeJavaStyleString` method:

```java
private static String escapeJavaStyleString(String str, boolean escapeSingleQuotes) {
    if (str == null) {
        return null;
    }
    try {
        StringWriter writer = new StringWriter(str.length() * 2);
        escapeJavaStyleString(writer, str, escapeSingleQuotes);
        return writer.toString();
    } catch (IOException ioe) {
        // Exception handling
        ioe.printStackTrace();
        return null;
    }
}
```

To fix the issue where '/' characters are being incorrectly escaped in version 2.4 of Commons Lang, the following method should be fixed:

`org.apache.commons.lang.StringEscapeUtils.escapeJavaStyleString(java.lang.String, boolean)`

This is the method where the logic for escaping characters, including the logic for '/' characters, is implemented. Fixing this method to correctly handle the escaping of characters will address the bug reported in the failing test.

